<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory X</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1210–<a href="/years/1276">1276</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory X was pope from 1271 to 1276, elected after a vacancy of almost three years. He held the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1274, which introduced the strict conclave rules for papal elections, and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> as king. In 1274 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a> handed him the Comtat Venaissin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory X" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eccekevin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gregory_X,_Buffalmacco,_Arezzo_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teobaldo Visconti was born around 1210 in Piacenza. He began his career in the household of the Cistercian Cardinal Giacomo di Pecorari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Palestrina, whom he served as majordomo and accompanied on legations, and he became a canon of Piacenza and of the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>. After the cardinal's death in 1244 he settled in Lyons and helped organise the council that met there in 1245, where he met Bonaventure and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>.</p><p>In 1246 he was appointed Archdeacon in the diocese of Liège and was told to preach a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. His time there was unhappy, and he left Liège, spending years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he became a friend of King Louis IX. Pope Clement IV later sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he befriended Prince Edward, the future Edward I, and went with him on crusade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The cardinals, divided between French and Italian parties at Viterbo, chose him on 1 September 1271 after he was proposed by a committee of six. He was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> on the Ninth Crusade when the news came. Not yet a priest, he was ordained on 19 March 1272 and crowned on 27 March at St Peter's Basilica. At his last sermon in Acre he quoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a>: if I forget thee, O Jerusalem, let my right hand forget her cunning.</p><p>The Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> met on 1 May 1274 to consider the schism between East and West, the condition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and abuses in the Church. To finance a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> he imposed on all clerics a tax of 10 per cent of income for six years. In the constitution Ubi periculum, ratified on 16 July 1274, he laid down the rules of the conclave: the cardinals shut in together, two thirds of votes needed, and one dish a day if no pope was chosen after three days. The rules were briefly annulled by Adrian V and John XXI but stood until the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 7 October 1272 he issued the bull Vineam Sorec, which condemned violence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and forced baptism and rejected the blood libel. He also received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>'s letter carried by the Polos and an embassy of the Mongol Ilkhanate leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a>, but plans for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> ended with his death.</p><p>Ill with a hernia, he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> on 10 January 1276 on his way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and is buried in its cathedral. Pope Clement XI beatified him in 1713.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Gregory X” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory X</b> quoted it when he left the Crusade upon his election in the papal conclave of 1268–1271.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory X</b> convoked a general council of the Church to discuss reunion of the Churches, a crusade to the Holy Land and Church reform.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-31">31 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Paris of 1229 after the Albigensian Crusade, Raymond VII ceded it to the Church, but the French crown held it until Philip III handed it to <b>Pope Gregory X</b> in 1274.</blockquote><a href="/state/comtat-venaissin">Comtat Venaissin</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gothic cathedral of Lausanne was consecrated in 1275 in the presence of <b>Pope Gregory X</b> and King Rudolf I. The townspeople gradually won rights of self-government.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lausanne">Prince-Bishopric of Lausanne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Piacenza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=1252" aria-label="Piacenza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4781 573 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Gregory X"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5231 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5231 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=1252">What was here in 1252? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
